{"parentUuid":"cf41e7f5-000b-49f3-ba78-0dea08a510b4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_012fZDGUR5skaecBMZsbpvbo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Check 1: which `entire-agent-kiro.exe` is actually winning the PATH lookup\n\n```powershell\nwhere.exe entire-agent-kiro\n```\n\nThis prints every match on PATH **in resolution order**. Whichever path comes first is the one being used. If you see multiple, the others are dead weight or stale.\n\n## Check 2: hash-match the resolved binary against the build\n\nThe exact file I built has SHA256:\n```\n0b8af76bf267c9288d3dd96574e48be7003745eec6b72536a622c4a81f4bd20d\n```\n\nOn Windows, after copying it to whatever path Check 1 reported as the winning location:\n```powershell\ncertutil -hashfile \"C:\\full\\path\\to\\entire-agent-kiro.exe\" SHA256\n```\n\nIf the hash matches the one above, that exact file is what `entire-agent-kiro` resolves to. If not, the copy didn't land where you thought, or another copy is shadowing it earlier in PATH — fix the path order or replace the right file.\n\n## Check 3: behavioral fingerprint\n\nOld binary emits bare or `sh -c` style hook commands. New binary emits `cmd /c \"<cmd> <NUL\"`. So:\n\n```powershell\ncd <test-repo>\nentire enable kiro --force\ntype .kiro\\hooks\\entire-stop.kiro.hook\n```\n\nIf you see `\"command\": \"cmd /c \\\"entire hooks kiro stop <NUL\\\"\"` — the new binary is running. If you see anything else (`\"command\": \"entire hooks kiro stop\"` or `\"command\": \"sh -c '...\"`) — the old binary is still in play and Check 1/2 will tell you why.\n\nTip: `where.exe` is the canonical answer; the other two checks just confirm the *content* at that path is what you think it is."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1025,"cache_read_input_tokens":211288,"output_tokens":594,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_f