{"parentUuid":"c65d32ef-ab4a-489d-b1e4-9a8cc866c64b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013jHJe4p2b2mRyD4FnVS2Ld","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entire Recall:\n\n## Closest precedent\nOriginal transcript-offset trim added to CLI/SQLite path, then extended to IDE path the same day — checkpoint `4957b6496f2c`, 2026-03-21, Alisha Kawaguchi (commit `95198c5`)\n\n## What worked\n- Tracked `{conversation_id, position}` in `.entire/tmp/kiro-transcript-offset.json`. After each capture, store `position = len(history)`. Next capture trims `history[position:]` so each checkpoint's `full.jsonl` is self-contained instead of cumulative.\n- Reset trimming automatically when `conversation_id` changes (new Kiro session) — keyed comparison, not unconditional reset.\n- Picked Go-side trim over SQL-level slicing (e.g. `json_extract(value, '$.history[N:]')`) because both still need the same offset state and Go is more testable.\n- Switched `ensureCachedTranscript` to query Kiro's SQLite by `conversation_id` rather than CWD, for precise per-session targeting.\n- Added e2e `ValidateCheckpointDeep` to catch empty/missing transcripts in lifecycle tests.\n\n## Gotchas\n- IDE path was missed in the first pass. Fixed same day in follow-up commit `73b7e4a` (checkpoint `2005d4a7117b`) — `ensureIDETranscript` was never calling `trimTranscriptHistory`, even though `parseTranscript` auto-converts IDE→CLI internally so the same trim function works.\n- Atomicity concern raised in the original session: offset is updated **before** the checkpoint is durably written. If a stop event captures, updates the offset, then the checkpoint write fails, the next stop will under-include. The shipped code accepts this trade-off.\n- IDE format has no `conversation_id` field — `convertIDETranscript` produces an empty string. So the `prev.ConversationID == parsed.ConversationID` guard is `\"\" == \"\"` for IDE paths, which means the trim never resets across true session boundaries on the IDE path. (This is exactly the kind of edge case that produces today's `{null, assistant}` orphan output when an exec-log enrichment expands a single IDE turn into multiple paired entries that straddle the offset.)\n\n## Files touched\n- `agents/entire-agent-kiro/internal/kiro/transcript.go` (both commits)\n- `agents/entire-agent-kiro/internal/kiro/transcript_test.go` (both commits)\n- `agents/entire-agent-kiro/internal/kiro/hooks.go` (initial)\n- `e2e/lifecycle_test.go` (initial — `ValidateCheckpointDeep`)\n\n## Suggested approach for your task\nThe orphaned `{user=null, assistant=…}` entry you saw in the Windows output traces directly to this trim logic. The trim runs on already-paired CLI-format entries; when `enrichIDETranscriptWithExecutionLogs` expands a turn into multiple paired entries (one with the user attached, then 1+ assistant-only entries from the action stream), the stored `position` can land mid-run, leaving an orphan assistant on the next capture. To investigate further, start in `transcript.go:302-338` (`trimTranscriptHistory`) and `transcript.go:1069+` (`enrichIDETranscriptWithExecutionLogs` — note the `assistantEntries[1:]` append at line ~1100). The reproducer template is the test repo described in checkpoint `4957b6496f2c`'s prompt: a multi-turn Kiro session with checkpoints, then assert each turn's `full.jsonl` is delta-only.\n\n## Other relevant precedents\n- checkpoint `2005d4a7117b` — IDE-path trim follow-up (same day, same author): the IDE branch fix that depends on the original trim helper."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1723,"cache_read_input_tokens":318925,"output_tokens":1883,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1723,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"ou